From 145aea77a127d42861887565552cb52fd142e4b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 09:45:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 047/109] drm/sprd: add a takeover driver for the SC7730/SC8830
 DISPC

The 32-bit Spreadtrum SoCs ("shark") have a display controller that
shares no register layout with the SharkL3 DPU of sprd_dpu.c. The
bootloader brings up DISPC, the DesignWare DSI host, the D-PHY and the
panel and scans out its splash buffer through the OSD layer in DPI
video mode. Without DSI and panel drivers that pipeline cannot be
restarted, so this driver keeps it running and only moves the OSD base
address to its own buffers, as the vendor kernel does at its splash
takeover:

- probe checks the bootloader's gates and the expected DISPC state
  before anything else, derives the mode from the DPI timing and pixel
  clock registers and sends simpledrm away;
- a flip writes OSD_BASE_ADDR and requests the register latch at the
  next VSYNC (DPI_CTRL bit 5); UPDATE_DONE completes the page flip,
  VSYNC drives vblank, with a 100 ms guard against a missing latch;
- CMA buffers are mapped cached and flushed before each flip, as for
  software rendering every blend would otherwise read uncached memory;
- disabling the CRTC switches the backlight off and points the layer
  at a private black buffer, since DISPC keeps scanning out.

On the Galaxy Tab E 9.6 (SM-T560) 1210 vsync-locked flips took 20.02 s,
60.5 Hz with no missed frame, matching the panel's 60.45 Hz.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
